Let~$C_n$ be the cycle graph on~$n$ vertices, with
vertices~$v_1,\dots,v_n$ around the cycle.  Take~$q=v_n$. It is well-known
that~$\Jac(C_n)\simeq\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$, with generator~$D_1:=[v_1-q]$ and such
that $D_j:=[v_{j}-q]=j[v_1-q]$ for all~$j$, where the indices are determined
modulo~$n$.  Therefore,~$\ord_{q}(v_i)=n/\gcd(i,n)$ for all~$i$.  For
convenience, take~$\ell_{v_i}=n$ for~$i=1,\dots,n-1$ and~$\ell_q=1$.  The reduced
Laplacian~$\tilde{L}$ is the~$(n-1)\times(n-1)$ tridiagonal matrix with~$2$s on the
diagonal and~$-1$s on the super and subdiagonals.  It is straightforward to
reduce~$\tilde{L}$ to its Hermite form, which is~$I_{n-1}+B_{n-1}$ where~$I_{n-1}$ is the
identity matrix and~$B_{n-1}$ is a matrix whose rows are all~$0$-vectors except for
the last row, which is the vector~$(1,2,\dots,n-1)$.  See Figure~\ref{fig:
ps-cycle} for an example.
